---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import SunMoonCompass from '../../components/SunMoonCompass.astro';
import PageHero from '../../components/PageHero.astro';
import RelatedTools from '../../components/RelatedTools.astro';
---

<Layout
	title="Sun & Moon Direction on the Compass — Live Azimuth"
	description="Find the sun and moon direction from the compass diagram. Live sensor heading with the sun's azimuth and the moon's azimuth plotted on the dial for any location."
	path="/es/sun-moon/"
	keywords="sun direction, moon direction, sun azimuth, moon azimuth, find sun on compass, sun position, moon position"
>
	<section class="relative overflow-hidden">
		<div class="mesh-gradient" aria-hidden="true"></div>
		<div class="relative">
			<PageHero
				eyebrow="sun & moon direction"
				title="Watch the sun and moon on the compass."
				description="A live phone compass with the sun's azimuth and the moon's azimuth plotted straight onto the dial. Face the marker, and you're looking at the sun or moon — no guessing."
			>
				<SunMoonCompass />
			</PageHero>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="border-y border-hairline bg-canvas">
		<div class="mx-auto max-w-[1200px] grid gap-6 px-4 py-16 sm:px-6 md:grid-cols-3">
			<div>
				<p class="eyebrow mb-2">how to read it</p>
				<p class="text-sm text-body leading-6">
					The red needle points north. Two colored dots on the dial show the compass direction
					of the sun (amber) and the moon (blue). Turn until you face a dot to look straight at it.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div>
				<p class="eyebrow mb-2">live updates</p>
				<p class="text-sm text-body leading-6">
					The sun and moon markers reposition automatically as the day and night progress. Use
					your location so the azimuths match the sky above you.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div>
				<p class="eyebrow mb-2">direction, not just numbers</p>
				<p class="text-sm text-body leading-6">
					Azimuths are absolute directions. The dial overlays them on your real heading, so you
					can find the sun or moon instantly, even behind the clouds.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="border-t border-hairline bg-canvas">
		<div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-20 sm:px-6 md:py-24">
			<p class="eyebrow">the sun & moon direction tool</p>
			<h2 class="mt-3 text-display-lg text-ink">Find the sun and moon direction from the compass diagram</h2>

			<p class="mt-6 text-body-lg text-body">
				This page puts the
				<strong class="text-ink font-medium">sun direction</strong> and
				<strong class="text-ink font-medium">moon direction</strong> directly onto a live compass
				diagram. Wherever the amber or blue marker sits on the dial is the compass direction you
				should face to look at the sun or the moon. It is the fastest way to
				<strong class="text-ink font-medium">find the sun on a compass</strong> or spot the moon
				in daylight — no astronomy knowledge required.
			</p>
			<p class="mt-4 leading-7">
				Azimuth is measured clockwise from true north: 0° is north, 90° east, 180° south, and
				270° west. The tool computes the live azimuth of both bodies for your coordinates and
				draws them on the dial alongside your real device heading, so the diagram always points
				you in the right direction.
			</p>

			<h3 class="mt-10 text-display-sm text-ink">Reading the sun and moon on the dial</h3>
			<p class="mt-3 leading-7">
				The <strong class="text-ink font-medium">amber marker</strong> is the sun and the
				<strong class="text-ink font-medium">blue marker</strong> is the moon. Each shows the
				body's compass bearing in degrees and as a compass point, along with its elevation above
				the horizon. When a marker points off the dial or reads a low elevation, that body is
				below the horizon — it is under your feet, not in view.
			</p>
			<p class="mt-4 leading-7">
				The sun rises in the east and sets in the west, so its marker sweeps across the northern
				half of the dial (in the northern hemisphere) from morning to evening. The moon follows a
				similar path but shifts roughly 12 degrees later each day, which is why its position on
				the compass changes from one day to the next.
			</p>

			<h3 class="mt-10 text-display-sm text-ink">Why use the compass to find the sun and moon</h3>
			<p class="mt-3 leading-7">
				A compass direction is far more practical than a set of sky coordinates. Whether you are
				photographing, planning shade, aligning a satellite dish, checking where light will fall,
				or just curious which way the moon is tonight, seeing the direction as a point on the
				compass makes it instantly actionable — turn, and there it is.
			</p>
			<p class="mt-4 leading-7">
				Everything runs locally in your browser and no data is uploaded. Combined with the
				<a href="/es/sun/" class="text-link underline decoration-link/30 hover:decoration-link">sun position calculator</a>
				and the
				<a href="/es/compass/" class="text-link underline decoration-link/30 hover:decoration-link">online compass</a>,
				this gives you the full picture of what is above you, wherever you are.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<RelatedTools current="/sun-moon/" />
</Layout>
